"compared with U.S. adults without epilepsy, adults with active or inactive epilepsy were more likely to report an inability to afford prescription medicine\"},{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"Selected subgroups of adults with active or inactive epilepsy (e.g., those aged <65 years and in a family earning <200%\"}]},\"inference\":{\"v\":\"CIs and tests\",\"method_stated\":true,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"All reported differences are statistically significant (p<0.05 by two-tailed t-tests).\"},{\"loc\":\"table 1\",\"q\":\"% (95% CI) › Active epilepsy † (n = 735) › Age-standardized\"}]},\"primary_measures\":[{\"v\":\"age-standardized prevalence (%)\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 1\",\"q\":\"Crude and age-standardized prevalences* of indicators of limitations in access to care\"}]},{\"v\":\"crude prevalence (%)\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 1\",\"q\":\"% (95% CI) › Active epilepsy † (n = 735) › Crude\"}]},{\"v\":\"age-standardized percentage\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Numbers and age-standardized percentages * of living in a family having problems paying medical bills\"}]}],\"regression_fields\":null,\"public_health_question\":{\"v\":\"Do U.S. adults with active or inactive epilepsy face more barriers to accessing and affording health care than adults without epilepsy?\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"To obtain updated estimates of access to health care among U.S. adults aged ≥18 years by epilepsy status\"}]},\"conditions\":[{\"v\":\"epilepsy\",\"role\":\"exposure\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"title\",\"q\":\"Barriers to and Disparities in Access to Health Care Among Adults Aged ≥18 Years with Epilepsy\"}]}],\"study_design\":[{\"v\":\"cross-sectional\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"First, because NHIS is cross-sectional, causal inferences related to the association between health care access barriers and epilepsy status cannot be made.\"}]}],\"data_source_type\":[{\"v\":\"national survey\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"CDC analyzed pooled data from the 2015 and 2017 National Health Interview Survey (NHIS)\"}]}],\"data_source_named\":[{\"v\":\"National Health Interview Survey (NHIS)\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"CDC analyzed pooled data from the 2015 and 2017 National Health Interview Survey (NHIS)\"}]}],\"setting\":{\"v\":\"US national\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"NHIS is an annual, nationally representative household survey of the U.S. civilian, noninstitutionalized population.\"}]},\"vaccine_outcome_type\":null,\"equity\":{\"level\":2,\"level_reason\":\"Outcomes are access and affordability measures (SDOH-type), and Table 2 reports them by age, sex, race/ethnicity, poverty and education; but contrasts are shown only as percentages with t-test significance marks, never as computed differences or ratios, so the cap at 2 applies.\",\"level_confidence\":\"medium\",\"level_alternative\":{\"level\":4,\"level_reason_note\":null,\"reason\":\"The title frames disparities, the outcomes are social determinants (problems paying medical bills), and Medicaid benefits, transportation, provider availability and public insurance reimbursement are named as explanations.\"},\"dimensions\":[{\"v\":\"age\",\"role\":\"population\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"title\",\"q\":\"Barriers to and Disparities in Access to Health Care Among Adults Aged ≥18 Years with Epilepsy\"}]},{\"v\":\"age\",\"role\":\"covariate\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"Estimates were weighted and age-standardized to the 2000 U.S. Census Bureau projected adult population using three age groups: 18–44, 45–64, and ≥65 years.\"}]},{\"v\":\"age\",\"role\":\"stratifier\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Age group, yrs › 18–44\"}]},{\"v\":\"sex\",\"role\":\"stratifier\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Sex › Women\"}]},{\"v\":\"race/ethnicity\",\"role\":\"stratifier\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Race/Ethnicity › Black, non-Hispanic\"},{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"Although all racial and ethnic groups with active epilepsy were more likely to report having problems paying medical bills than their counterparts without epilepsy\"}]},{\"v\":\"SES\",\"role\":\"stratifier\",\"ses_components\":[\"poverty status\",\"education level\",\"current employment\"],\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Poverty status §§§ › <100% (of FPL)\"},{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Education level › Less than HS graduate\"}]}],\"determinants_of_health\":{\"v\":\"problems paying medical bills and inability to afford care (analyzed outcomes); reliable transportation; provider availability or cultural competency; lower public insurance reimbursement; Medicaid benefit coverage\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"summary\",\"q\":\"or were in families having problems paying medical bills.\"},{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"contextual factors that constitute social determinants of health (e.g., reliable transportation, provider availability or cultural competency, and lower rates of public insurance reimbursement)\"}]},\"equity_action\":{\"v\":false,\"ev\":[]},\"equity_measurement\":\"Referent is adults without epilepsy within each sociodemographic level; insurance type (Medicaid vs private) is an outcome. Epilepsy is treated as a clinical condition, not disability. 'Other' race is suppressed for inactive epilepsy.\"},\"groupwise\":[{\"across\":[\"SES\"],\"n_groups\":5,\"ordered\":true,\"assessment\":\"side by side\",\"resolves_to_finer\":false,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Poverty status §§§ › ≥400%\"}]},{\"across\":[\"race/ethnicity\"],\"n_groups\":4,\"ordered\":false,\"assessment\":\"side by side\",\"resolves_to_finer\":false,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Race/Ethnicity › Hispanic\"}]},{\"across\":[\"SES\"],\"n_groups\":3,\"ordered\":true,\"assessment\":\"side by side\",\"resolves_to_finer\":false,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 2\",\"q\":\"Education level › Some college or more\"}]}],\"uncertain_fields\":[\"comparison\",\"groupwise\",\"equity.level\",\"analytic_depth\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"notes\":\"Two pairwise contrasts (active vs none, inactive vs none) are read as framework pair. Table 2 tests epilepsy vs no epilepsy within each sociodemographic level, which is stratification, not a test across SPD levels. Design named 'cross-sectional' only in the limitations.\",\"view_issues\":\"Table 1 note gives age groups 18–34, 35–64, ≥65 but the text and Table 2 give 18–44, 45–64, ≥65. Table 1 eyeglasses CI is garbled ('9.7-–5.9'). The inactive-uninsured crude CI (10.6–15.4) looks implausible for 10.9.\"}", "type": "text"}]}